
= Background
This section presents relevant research and frameworks to establish* an overview of research on therapist interfaces for rehabilitation through exergames.* The chapter covers the following areas: (1) Exergames & Automation in Rehabilitation, (2) More-than-Human Agency, and (3) Therapist Interfaces for Exergame-Based Therapy.


== Exergames & Automation in Rehabilitation
Exergames, a fusion of "exercise" and "video games", are video games that promote physical activity and cognitive engagement through gamified tasks @ohyou2010. Although not exclusively, they are often classified under a broader category "Serious Games", which refers to digital games designed primarily for purposes beyond entertainments, such as education, training, and health interventions @Maggio2025. The researches also differentiate between commercial off-the-shelf systems and custom systems used for rehabilitation purposes @Allegue2022. The meta-analyses show exergames systems specifically designed for rehabilitation tend to be more effective than conventional therapy, whereas re-purposed commercial off-the-shelf systems often yield mixed or neutral results, in part because their interaction mechanics and feedback are not tightly aligned with therapeutic goals @Maier2019. *Exergames* have shown to support neuroplasticity by enabling high repetition and high intensity task-oriented activities that are challenging enough but not frustrating resulting in increased motivation and engagement  @Maier2019 @Allegue2022 @Shah2022. 

A wide range of technologies have been explored to implement exergames for rehabilitation. Non-immersive systems based on Xbox Kinect, Nintendo Wii, PlayStation, Robot-assisted serious games, or similar motion-sensing consoles are widely studied and have shown be be clinically provide effective rehabilitation compared to conventional therapy @Cheok2015 @Santos2025 @Doumas2025. Similarly, XR-based exergames, which are more immersive in nature, have been used for motor and cognitive rehabilitation after stroke and other neurological conditions, with studies reporting improvements in motor functions, spatial attention, and improved engagement compared to conventional exercises @Fordell2016 @Faria2016. Additionally, automation of various work processes is increasingly transforming modern workplaces in rehabilitation, with many functions now being carried out either partially or fully by automated systems @TurmoVidal2025 @Yu2026. These automated data-driven systems support personalized rehabilitation, and reduce therapists workload while preserving the human aspects and judgement. Exergame platforms with recommendation systems automatically select and plan personalized exercises based on performance histories, biofeedback and other data points, shifting agency from therapist to algorithms @Ismail2021 @Zhao2020. Similarly, XR-exergames use biofeedback, emotion recognition, and real-time performance analytics to adjust games difficult while therapists interfaces are treated as secondary tools for configuring high-level therapy goals and evaluation. These studies demonstrate that exergames can be implemented and are being explored in varied technologies including across different rehabilitation settings while automation in the these systems are enhancing the work process to provide personalized rehabilitation. 

These new developments are often discussed using the notions of human-in-the-loop (HITL), and human-on-the-loop (HOTL). In HITL, humans are embedded in the control loop and must initiate, approve or adjust system decisions before they are executed or can move forward in the workflow. For example, when a therapists manually selects the exergames and its parameters or when a therapists manually checks and approves the exergames suggested by a recommendation system. In HOTL, the system operates autonomously while human monitors and intervene when required. For example, when an exergame system continuously adjusts game parameters based on patient performance or biofeedback while the therapist supervises through their own interface @Lazaros2026. Classic Level of Automation (LoA) taxonomies present how functions are divided between human and AI @AitMalek2020. These frameworks are tools to understand implications and challenges within each level of collaboration in a socio-technical environment like clinical context @Ruscio2024. More recent frameworks like GALA framework, emphasize that humans are increasingly supervising semi-autonomous systems rather than directly manipulating every action. As mentioned previously, many rehabilitation technologies already embody these implicit choices even when they are not described in the same terms.

