== More-than-Human Agency 

From more-than-human perspective in HCI, computational systems can be viewed as co-creators of actions, phenomena, and experiences, and not just as tools "used by" humans @Frauenberger2019. 

Computational systems are no longer just tools "used by" humans, rather they are co-creators of actions, phenomena and experiences @Frauenberger2019. In relation to these systems, humans are not acting alone. The fourth wave emphasizes the need to move away from the notion of human-centered design where humans are *autonomous* decision-makers interacting with neutral tools, or view computational systems as autonomous tools that simply replace humans. Instead*, the 4th wave* view them as co-performers where humans and computational systems continuously shape each other and perform social practices together @Frauenberger2019 @Kuijer2018. As such, agency is not something human posses individually or independently, but rather relational and distributed across relationships between humans, technologies, institutions, environments, and norms. 

Therefore, from a more-than-human perspective, agency in rehabilitation can be presented as distributed across humans (therapists and patients) and non-humans (such as exergame systems, algorithms, interfaces, sensors, data representations, institutions, and even regulatory frameworks, rather than being possessed by any single actor @GiaccardiRedstrom2020 @Frauenberger2019. As these system become more data-driven, healthcare decisions are increasingly influenced by algorithmic logic and historical data apart from just therapist reasoning @Jones2023. This means, in the context of exergame-based rehabilitation, therapy outcomes would be shaped not only by therapists' decisions and patients' performance, but also by how algorithms classify performance, how data is presented, what data is shown or hidden, what technology is used, and even institutional and legal constraints. This reiterates the fact that design in therapeutic scenarios is more about configuring relationships and agencies among multiple human and nonhuman actors over time, rather than just optimizing features within an interface. 

The sense of agency can be affected by factors such as timing, predictability, and level or form of automation @Legaspi2024; @Limerick2014. Experimental studies indicate that higher level of automation and increased cognitive workload are associated with reduced sense of agency, whereas thoughtfully designed hybrid interactions can maintain a stronger feeling of control even when system is acting on user's behalf @Howard2016 @Legaspi2024. These findings are particularly relevant in clinical contexts where therapists must be able to stand by decisions, both ethically and legally, even when those clinical decisions are mediated by automated systems. It is emphasized that clinicians should still be able to intervene when co-performing with AI, because legal and ethical responsibility for decisions currently remains with them @Olawade2026. The human agency is thus not replaced by AI because it is central to the process and continuously negotiated with the system @Frauenberger2019. Although, delegating more decision-making to the autonomous systems can create power imbalance. Additionally, there is a positive correlation between frequent reliance on AI tools and reduced clinical reasoning or decision-making skills and actions @Gerlich2025. For this reason, a more balanced approach where systems "performs" but the judgement of what is good, bad, or appropriate ultimately remains with the humans is recommended @Kuijer2018.

== Therapist Interfaces for Exergame-Based Therapy - phases <therapistinterfaces>
Various therapist interfaces for personalized rehabilitation through exergames have been developed, although they do not account for the entirety of the therapy process as they mainly focus on data documentation and/or session planning and task manipulation. One therapist interface for Village Druid VR, a therapy game used for rehabilitation was prototyped along through 2 co-design sessions with 2 therapists each. The lo-fi and hi-fi prototypes focused on patient data documentation for monitoring and progress evaluation as well as some simpler personalization session parameters which were not clearly defined @Ferreira2022. Similarly, #cite(<Duval2022>, form: "prose") redesigned an already existing conventional VR game, Spellcasters, through co-design sessions with 14 medical professionals (PT's & OT's). The re-purposed version of Spellcasters focused again solely on data documentation deemed valuable by the medical professionals, and task management through custom rehabilitative gestures to adjust relevant variables including scale, shape, direction and depth.

Another more elaborative study developed a set of collaborative VR serious games, 4 in total, both for upper-limb physical rehabilitation and cognitive rehabilitation of stroke survivors. Again, their framework ADAPT (Adaptive Dynamics for Activity-based Personalized Therapy) only focused on personalized session planning and some game task parameters. For the session planning, therapists could adjust the number of participants, the selection and sequencing of games, session durations and individualized goals. For game task parameters, the therapist could adjust level of difficulty and more detailed adjustments in complexity such as the number and type of objects or puzzle pieces, and in-game payment processes @Figueiredo2025-ADAPT. 

One study in particular fully isolated game task adjustment in the form of difficulty parameters only. Difficulty in classification in levels were found to lead to longer playtime with more fun and higher performance when compared to high-commitment game parameter goals in terms of goal and time @Eckert2023. #cite(<GagnonRoy2025>, form: "prose"), on the other hand co-developed COOK, a cognitive assistance system together with occupational therapists to help patients regain the ability to prepare meals. Although not an exergame, the patient still has a personalized interface with step-by-step instructions to follow where breaks, reminders and recipe-steps are all pre-configured by the therapist. 

With one exception, previous research does not account for how to share the agency between the therapist and the system. #cite(<Figueiredo2025HybridControl>, form: "prose"), the exception, highlight in their study that 7 out of 10 participants preferred a hybrid mode combining both automated and manual adjustments, however focused on task personalization only as well. Furthermore, while data documentation does improve evaluation and decision-making and personalized therapy and decision-making, ultimately improving patient care @Tadas2023 @Dowding2015, the current systems still remain very fragmented as they lack a holistic view of all the main phases in the therapeutic process. The Occupational Intervention Process model includes (1) Evaluation and Goal-Setting Phase, (2) Intervention Phase, (3) Reevaluation Phase @Hinojosa_Kramer_Royeen_2017 @fisher2009occupational. Physiotherapy largely uses the same basic structure and is often referred to as the "rehab cycle" or "SOAP" which also includes (1) Assessment and goal setting, (2) intervention and (3) re-evaluation @Negrini2019 @Physiopedia. 

Existing rehabilitation systems are fragmented across different platforms for assessment, therapy, and documentation. Furthermore, the current therapists interfaces for exergame rehabilitation system primarily focuses on adjustable parameters, dashboards, automated adjustments, effective recommendations, rather than on how control and responsibility are shared between therapists and computation systems across the full therapeutic process. 








